<p>The traditional and prevailing scholarly view holds that Romanians are descendants of Romanized Dacians who persisted on their native soil despite invasions and migrations spanning several centuries. This continuity theory argues that the Latin language, infused by the Romans during their roughly 170-year presence, survived barbarian incursions—be it Goths, Huns, Slavs, or Avars—through pockets of resilient rural populations. Over time, this Latin base evolved into the Romanian language, a Romance language geographically isolated from its Western cousins by Slavic, Hungarian, and Turkic speakers. This hypothesis enjoys considerable support from linguistic evidence: Romanian retains Latin grammatical structures that point to an early, localized Roman influence rather than later Latin-mediated introduction.</p>

