Certainly! I'll provide a detailed step-by-step guide to set up a modern NixOS environment using flakes and Home Manager, starting from a fresh NixOS installation.

**Goal:**
- Use flakes-based Nix configuration
- Use GNOME on Wayland
- Have neovim, VS Code, Zed editor, Brave browser, Bitwarden
- Install 10 standard tools like `curl`, `git`, `htop`, etc.
- Manage user configuration via Home Manager

---

# Step 1: Fresh NixOS installation

Assuming you have installed NixOS with the minimal system and have rebooted into your system. You are logged in as `root` or a non-root user.

Make sure Nix is functional by running:

```bash
nix-env --version
```

---

# Step 2: Enable flakes support globally

By default, flakes support is experimental and must be enabled.

Edit the Nix daemon config:

```bash
sudo mkdir -p /etc/nix
echo "experimental-features = nix-command flakes" | sudo tee -a /etc/nix/nix.conf
```

Or if `/etc/nix/nix.conf` exists, append `experimental-features = nix-command flakes`.

---

# Step 3: Create your nixos flake config repository

We'll place our NixOS config under `/etc/nixos` or in your home directory and then link it as a flake.

Let's create a flake repo at `/etc/nixos` to manage system config.

```bash
sudo mkdir -p /etc/nixos
cd /etc/nixos
sudo nix-shell -p git --run "git init"
```

Alternatively, do it as your user and then move files or adjust permissions.

---

# Step 4: Your NixOS flake skeleton

Create these files in `/etc/nixos`:

### `flake.nix`

```nix
{
  description = "NixOS configuration with flakes and Home Manager";

  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-23.05";  # or nixos-unstable if you prefer
    home-manager = {
      url = "github:nix-community/home-manager";
      inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
    };
    flake-utils.url = "github:numtide/flake-utils";
  };

  outputs = {nixpkgs, home-manager, flake-utils, ...}:
    flake-utils.lib.eachDefaultSystem (system:
      let
        pkgs = nixpkgs.legacyPackages.${system};
        userName = "yourusername"; # change to your actual username
        userHome = "/home/${userName}";
      in {
        nixosConfigurations.yourhostname = nixpkgs.lib.nixosSystem {
          system = system;
          modules = [
            ({
              imports = [ home-manager.nixosModules.home-manager ];

              # Basic system config
              networking.hostName = "yourhostname"; # Set your hostname
              time.timeZone = "America/New_York";  # Change as appropriate
              i18n.defaultLocale = "en_US.UTF-8";

              users.users.${userName} = {
                isNormalUser = true;
                extraGroups = [ "wheel" "networkmanager" ];
                home = userHome;
                shell = pkgs.zsh;  # optional
              };

              # Enable sudo wheel group
              security.sudo.wheelNeedsPassword = false;

              # Enable essential services
              services.networkmanager.enable = true;
              services.xserver.enable = false; # Disable X server, use Wayland only

              # Enable GNOME Desktop on Wayland
              services.gnome = {
                enable = true;
                wayland = true;
              };

              # Enable graphical login manager
              services.gdm.enable = true;

              environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [
                curl git htop net-tools wget unzip
                vim neovim zsh gnupg
                # Adding brave browser from nixpkgs
                brave brave-beta brave-nightly # Brave stable and beta/nightly as fallback
                bitwarden-cli
                # Optional: VS Code
                vscode
                # Zed is not in nixpkgs by default, we'll handle later using home-manager or overlays
              ];

              # Enable Home Manager integration
              home-manager.useGlobalPkgs = true;
              home-manager.useUserPackages = true;

              # Enable Wayland session for GDM
              system.stateVersion = "23.05"; # Adjust according to nixos version

            })
          ];
        };
        # Home Manager configuration for user
        homeConfigurations.${userName} = home-manager.lib.homeManagerConfiguration {
          pkgs = pkgs;
          system = system;
          username = userName;
          homeDirectory = userHome;

          # User config
          configuration = {
            programs.home-manager.enable = true;

            # Editors
            programs.neovim = {
              enable = true;
              # Extra plugins, or configs can be added here
            };

            programs.zsh = {
              enable = true;
              ohMyZsh = {
                enable = true;
                theme = "agnoster";
              };
            };

            programs.git.enable = true;

            # VS Code (via vs-code nixpkg)
            programs.vscode = {
              enable = true;
              package = pkgs.vscode;
              extensions = [
                "ms-python.python"
                "ms-vscode.cpptools"
                "eamodio.gitlens"
                "ms-azuretools.vscode-docker"
              ];
            };

            # Brave browser via nixpkgs
            programs.brave = {
              enable = true;
            };

            # Bitwarden CLI is installed system-wide, but if you want GUI alternatives, could add here

            # Enable Wayland session detection
            environment.variables = {
              XDG_SESSION_TYPE = "wayland";
            };

            # Add standard CLI tools to user PATH (if needed, usually inherited)
          };
        };
      }
    );
}
```

---

### Explanation:

- We define inputs: nixpkgs, home-manager, flake-utils.
- The outputs define:
  - a NixOS configuration under `nixosConfigurations.yourhostname`
  - a Home Manager user configuration `homeConfigurations.yourusername`
- GNOME + Wayland is enabled inside NixOS config with `services.gnome`.
- NetworkManager + GDM enabled.
- `brave`, `bitwarden-cli`, `vscode`, and standard CLI tools included as system packages.
- `home-manager` manages your user environment, including neovim and zsh.
- VS Code extensions defined.
- Note: Zed editor is not packaged in nixpkgs. We'll install it manually via Home Manager extraPackages in a moment.

---

# Step 5: Set username and hostname

Modify these variables in the `flake.nix`:

```nix
userName = "yourusername"; # your actual username here, e.g. "alice"
system.stateVersion = "23.05"; # your NixOS version
```

Make sure the user exists in NixOS config's users section.

---

# Step 6: Add support for `zed` editor via a Home Manager overlay (optional)

Since `zed` is not packaged in nixpkgs (as of this writing), we can add a Home Manager `home.packages` section to install `zed` manually.

One common way is to use the `programs.zed` attribute or just install the zed binary via a wrapper, for example:

```nix
home.packages = [
  (pkgs.stdenv.mkDerivation {
    pname = "zed";
    version = "0.21.6"; # adjust to latest
    src = pkgs.fetchurl {
      url = "https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/releases/download/v0.21.6/zed-0.21.6-x86_64-linux.zip";
      sha256 = "sha256-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"; # calculate or get from release
    };
    unpackPhase = "true";
    installPhase = ''
      mkdir -p $out/bin
      unzip $src -d $out/bin
      chmod +x $out/bin/zed
    '';
  })
];
```

You must download the exact sha256 of the zip archive. To get it, you can do:

```bash
nix-prefetch-url https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/releases/download/v0.21.6/zed-0.21.6-x86_64-linux.zip
```

and replace sha256 with that sum.

---

# Step 7: Apply the system configuration

Run the following to apply your system config:

```bash
sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos#yourhostname
```

Replace `yourhostname` with your actual hostname you configured.

This will build and switch the system to the flake-based configuration.

---

# Step 8: Initialize your user environment

Now, switch to your user (`su - yourusername`) and configure and activate Home Manager.

You can link your home-manager config too:

```bash
home-manager switch --flake /etc/nixos#yourusername
```

If `home-manager` command is not found, install it:

```bash
nix profile install "github:nix-community/home-manager"
```

or run via `nix run`:

```bash
nix run .#homeConfigurations.yourusername.activationPackage
```

---

# Step 9: Post-configuration manual steps

- After reboot, you should get GDM login on Wayland with GNOME.
- Verify `echo $XDG_SESSION_TYPE` outputs `wayland`.
- Verify `neovim`, `vscode`, `zed`, `brave`, etc. are installed.
- You can install additional VS Code extensions via config or manually.

---

# Step 10: Summary example for 10 standard tools to add in `environment.systemPackages`

Just examples for CLI tools to add in your flake:

```nix
environment.systemPackages = with pkgs; [
  curl git htop net-tools wget unzip vim neovim zsh gnupg tmux
  bash tree
  brave bitwarden-cli vscode
];
```

---

# Final notes

- Adjust `system.stateVersion` according to your NixOS version.
- You can create a personal Git repo for your flake config for clean version control.
- Overlays can be used for adding packages like `zed`, or add your own nix packaging.
- This setup favors reproducibility and modern declarative configuration.

---

If you want, I can provide a ready-to-use minimal working example in a GitHub repo-style format or a ZIP with all files. Just ask!

