Below is one acceptable solution in C. In this solution we use binary search to efficiently find the last index where the array element is strictly less than the target value. If no element is strictly less than the target, the function returns –1.

Here’s the code:

------------------------------------------------
#include <stdio.h>

// Function to find the index of the array element that is strictly less than target.
// Returns the largest index i for which arr[i] < target, or -1 if no such element exists.
int findIndex(int arr[], int n, int target) {
    int left = 0, right = n - 1;
    int ans = -1;  // This will hold the result index

    // Binary search loop
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        // If arr[mid] is strictly less than target, move search to the right half
        if (arr[mid] < target) {
            ans = mid;  // record current index as a candidate
            left = mid + 1;  // try to find a later index that still satisfies the condition
        }
        else {
            // Otherwise, target is <= arr[mid]; move search to the left half
            right = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return ans;
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9};  // example array (monotonically increasing)
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    int target = 7;  // example target value

    int idx = findIndex(arr, n, target);
    if(idx != -1)
        printf("The index of the element strictly less than %d is: %d\n", target, idx);
    else
        printf("No array element is strictly less than %d\n", target);

    return 0;
}
------------------------------------------------

Explanation:

1. We initialize left, right, and ans. The variable ans keeps track of the best index found so far (starting with –1 indicating no valid index has been found).

2. We perform a binary search. If arr[mid] is strictly less than the target, we update ans to mid and search in the right half (to see if there’s a later element that still satisfies the condition).

3. If arr[mid] is greater than or equal to target, we search the left half.

4. Finally, if an element was found, its index is returned; otherwise, we return –1.

This solution runs in O(log n) time, which is efficient for large sorted arrays.

