Below is one common culprit and some suggestions on how to fix your code.

──────────────────────────────
Problem: Using the Transform as a “start marker”
──────────────────────────────
You’re currently doing this in moveToCenter:
  startMarker = this.transform;
This means that startMarker isn’t a snapshot of the starting position—it’s a reference to the same Transform that’s moving. As your card’s transform moves, startMarker.position updates too. That explains why your interpolation seems off; the “start” position isn’t fixed.

──────────────────────────────
Fix: Store the Starting Position in a Vector3
──────────────────────────────
Instead of storing a reference to the Transform, you can capture the starting position in a Vector3. For example:
  Vector3 startPos = transform.position;
Then, when Lerp’ing you use this startPos instead of startMarker.position. This ensures that endPos remains interpolated from the original position rather than a moving one.

──────────────────────────────
Other Suggestions and Adjustments
──────────────────────────────
1. Calculate journeyAmount after updating the elapsed time.
  Currently you are doing:
    journeyAmount = distance / journeyLength;
    if (journeyAmount < 1f) { distance = (Time.time - startTime) * speed; … }
  It’s better to update the distance first and then compute the ratio like this:
    distance = (Time.time - startTime) * speed;
    journeyAmount = distance / journeyLength;

2. Coroutine Stopping
  Calling StopCoroutine(move()) inside stopAnimation isn’t ideal because move() is a method that returns an enumerator and you’re calling StartCoroutine(move()) without saving the reference. It’s better to do one of these:
   • Use a boolean flag to exit the while loop or let the while loop finish when journeyAmount >= 1
   • Save the Coroutine handle when you call StartCoroutine, and then stop using that handle if needed

──────────────────────────────
Revised Code Example
──────────────────────────────
Below is your code revised with these suggestions:

-------------------------------------------------
using System.Collections;
using UnityEngine;

public class moveCard : MonoBehaviour
{
    public storage Storage;
    public float speed = 20f;

    // Floating point variables for time and journey data
    private float startTime;
    private float journeyLength;

    // Instead of a Transform "marker", store the starting position here.
    private Vector3 startPos;
    private Vector3 endPos;

    public int dir = 1;

    void Awake() 
    {
        if (gameObject.name == "reference") 
        {
            Storage.movePos = transform.position;
        }
    }

    public void moveToCenter() 
    {
        // Capture the starting position now:
        startPos = transform.position;
        startTime = Time.time;

        // Compute the target/ending position only once
        endPos = new Vector3(Storage.movePos.x * dir, Storage.movePos.y, Storage.movePos.z);
        journeyLength = Vector3.Distance(startPos, endPos);

        // Start the movement coroutine.
        StartCoroutine(Move());
        // Optionally: if you want to force-stop the coroutine later,
        // it’s better to store the Coroutine handle.
        Invoke("StopAnimation", 2f);
    }

    IEnumerator Move() 
    {
        while (true) 
        {
            // Calculate the distance traveled so far and the percentage of journey completed    
            float distance = (Time.time - startTime) * speed;
            float journeyAmount = distance / journeyLength;

            // Clamp journeyAmount (if necessary) so it doesn't exceed 1
            if(journeyAmount < 1f)
            {
                transform.position = Vector3.Lerp(startPos, endPos, journeyAmount);
                transform.rotation = Quaternion.Lerp(Quaternion.Euler(transform.rotation.eulerAngles), Quaternion.Euler(Storage.rot), journeyAmount);
                yield return null;
            }
            else 
            {
                // Ensure we reach the end exactly.
                transform.position = endPos;
                transform.rotation = Quaternion.Euler(Storage.rot);
                yield break;
            }
        }
    } 

    private void StopAnimation() 
    {
        Debug.Log("Coroutine ended");
        // If you want to stop the coroutine from elsewhere,
        // consider keeping a reference to the started Coroutine.
    }
}
-------------------------------------------------

──────────────────────────────
Summary
──────────────────────────────
• Instead of holding a reference to transform (via startMarker), store the starting position in a Vector3.
• Update the interpolation (Lerp) using the stored start position and your computed endPos.
• Reorder your calculation of distance and journeyAmount so that you first compute the elapsed distance, then update the ratio.
• Consider using a flag or storing the Coroutine handle if you need more control over stopping the coroutine.

With these adjustments, endPos will remain fixed, and your card’s movement should interpolate as expected.

