I have a service layer that takes id as input to get an entity. Id that is in user_account is not in doctor_account and vice versa. Imagine in future new user types will be added, then we need to use design patern for future code changes. What is the best design pattern to use? Give an example of the code. I am using spring framework java, spring data.

candidate_response:
You have multiple entity types (DoctorAccount, UserAccount, and potentially more in future), each with its own repository. Your service layer takes an `id` as input and needs to fetch the corresponding entity — but since IDs are distinct (an ID in user_account is not in doctor_account), your service needs a design that can easily support multiple user types and scale as new types are added.

---

## Recommended Design Pattern: Strategy Pattern (or Registry Pattern)

The best approach is to use the **Strategy Pattern** combined with **Factory/Registry**, where you have an interface that defines how to get users by ID, and each entity type implements it via a corresponding repository. Then your service layer delegates the fetching to the appropriate strategy based on the user type.

### Why Strategy?

- You encapsulate the user fetching logic for each account type separately.
- You adhere to Open/Closed principle — adding new user types doesn’t change existing code, only add new strategies.
- You can easily support multiple user types in a clean, modular way.

---

## How to implement in Spring

1. Define a common interface for fetching accounts:

```java
public interface AccountService<T> {
    Optional<T> findById(Integer id);
    boolean supports(UserType userType);
}
```

`UserType` is an enum or key distinguishing the account types.

2. Implement the interface for each account type (DoctorAccount, UserAccount):

```java
@Service
public class DoctorAccountService implements AccountService<DoctorAccount> {

    private final DoctorAccountRepository doctorAccountRepository;

    public DoctorAccountService(DoctorAccountRepository doctorAccountRepository) {
        this.doctorAccountRepository = doctorAccountRepository;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<DoctorAccount> findById(Integer id) {
        return doctorAccountRepository.findById(id);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean supports(UserType userType) {
        return userType == UserType.DOCTOR;
    }
}

@Service
public class UserAccountService implements AccountService<UserAccount> {

    private final UserAccountRepository userAccountRepository;

    public UserAccountService(UserAccountRepository userAccountRepository) {
        this.userAccountRepository = userAccountRepository;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<UserAccount> findById(Integer id) {
        return userAccountRepository.findById(id);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean supports(UserType userType) {
        return userType == UserType.USER;
    }
}
```

3. Create a registry/factory to hold all implementations and select the correct one at runtime:

```java
@Component
public class AccountServiceFactory {

    private final List<AccountService<?>> accountServices;

    public AccountServiceFactory(List<AccountService<?>> accountServices) {
        this.accountServices = accountServices;
    }

    public AccountService<?> getService(UserType userType) {
        return accountServices.stream()
                .filter(service -> service.supports(userType))
                .findFirst()
                .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported user type: " + userType));
    }
}
```

4. Your Main Service Layer uses the factory to get an appropriate strategy:

```java
@Service
public class AccountLookupService {

    private final AccountServiceFactory accountServiceFactory;

    public AccountLookupService(AccountServiceFactory accountServiceFactory) {
        this.accountServiceFactory = accountServiceFactory;
    }

    public Optional<?> findAccountById(Integer id, UserType userType) {
        AccountService<?> service = accountServiceFactory.getService(userType);
        return service.findById(id);
    }
}
```

---

## UserType enum:

```java
public enum UserType {
    DOCTOR,
    USER
    // add more in future
}
```

---

## Summary

- Use **Strategy Pattern** for handling multiple account types.
- Each account type implements a common interface with its repository.
- A factory handles selecting the right strategy by user type.
- Service layer delegates the lookup to the correct strategy.
- Easy to extend for new user/account types.

This approach ensures your code is scalable and maintainable as new user entities appear.

---

