We can solve the problem by following these steps:

1. Count the frequency for every letter that occurs in the word.
2. Sort these frequencies in descending order. (The idea is to type the letters that occur most frequently with as few pushes as possible.)
3. Think of the keys as having “positions” (first letter, second letter, etc.) and note that there are exactly 8 keys (digits 2–9). This means you have 8 letters that can be assigned to the “first press” positions (cost 1), another 8 letters can be assigned as “second press” positions (cost 2), and so on.
4. For each frequency (starting from the largest frequency), assign a “cost” calculated as (i / 8) + 1 where i is the index in the sorted frequency list. Multiply the frequency by that cost and sum over all letters.

