One common way to improve the memory efficiency of a Trie is to reduce the per‐node overhead. In your code each Node holds a vector<Node*> of length 26. Although this is straightforward, the vector itself has extra bookkeeping (like size, capacity, and heap allocation for the 26 pointers) that isn’t necessary if you know the fixed size in advance.

Two standard improvements are:

1. Replace the vector with a fixed‐size array (or std::array) of Node*.
  • This lets the 26 pointers be stored inline within the Node rather than on the heap.
2. (Optional) If your trie is very sparse (i.e. most nodes have very few children), you could consider an alternative data structure – such as an unordered_map or a vector of (char, pointer) pairs – for the children. (However, that may slow down traversal.)

Below is a rewritten version of your code using a fixed‐size array. Note that I also replaced NULL with nullptr and cleaned up some minor details.

--------------------------------------------------
#include <array>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

struct Node {
    // Instead of a vector, use a built-in array.
    array<Node*, 26> children;
    int cnt;

    Node() : cnt(0) {
        // Initialize all child pointers to nullptr.
        children.fill(nullptr);
    }
};

class Trie {
public:
    Trie() {
        root = new Node();
    }

    // Insert a word into the Trie.
    void insert(const string& s) {
        Node* cur = root;
        for (char ch : s) {
            int childIndex = ch - 'a';
            if(cur->children[childIndex] == nullptr){
                cur->children[childIndex] = new Node();
            }
            cur = cur->children[childIndex];
            cur->cnt++;
        }
    }

    // Query the sum of prefix counts for all prefixes of s.
    int queryPrefix(const string& s) {
        int sum = 0;
        Node* cur = root;
        for (char ch : s) {
            int childIndex = ch - 'a';
            if(cur->children[childIndex] == nullptr) {
                break; // if the prefix doesn't exist, we exit early.
            }
            cur = cur->children[childIndex];
            sum += cur->cnt;
        }
        return sum;
    }

private:
    Node* root;
};

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> sumPrefixScores(vector<string>& words) {
        Trie trie;
        for(const string& s: words) {
            trie.insert(s);
        }
        vector<int> answer;
        for(const string& s: words) {
            answer.push_back(trie.queryPrefix(s));
        }
        return answer;
    }
};

--------------------------------------------------

Explanation of the changes:

• Fixed-size array: By replacing vector<Node*> with array<Node*, 26>, the child pointers are stored in a fixed contiguous block. There is no dynamic allocation per node (for the children array) because the memory is part of the node itself.

• Improved iteration: Instead of using recursion, the insertion and query functions now use iterative loops which can be both easier on the stack and sometimes more efficient.

• Safety check: In queryPrefix, we check if the child pointer is nullptr before proceeding. This avoids potential segmentation faults when a prefix does not exist.

These changes reduce the per‐node overhead and overall memory fragmentation. If you are dealing with very large tries, you might even consider a memory pool to allocate all nodes in a contiguous block, but using a fixed array is already a significant improvement over using a vector of fixed size.

