One common “fast strlen” implementation uses word‐sized loads (via size_t) and a bit‐trick to test whether any byte in a word is zero. The idea is to first align the pointer to a word boundary and then read words (instead of one byte at a time) and test for a zero byte using arithmetic rather than checking each byte individually.

Below is one acceptable implementation. (Note that the constants used depend on the word size; here we show how to support both 32‐bit and 64‐bit size_t.):

---------------------------------------------------
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <limits.h>

/* Set up constants based on the size of size_t.
   For a 32‐bit word, SIZE_MAX is 0xffffffffUL,
   and for a 64‐bit word, SIZE_MAX is 0xffffffffffffffffUL. */
#if SIZE_MAX == 0xffffffffUL
  #define ONES 0x01010101UL
  #define HIGHS 0x80808080UL
#elif SIZE_MAX == 0xffffffffffffffffUL
  #define ONES 0x0101010101010101UL
  #define HIGHS 0x8080808080808080UL
#else
  #error "Unsupported size_t size"
#endif

size_t fast_strlen(const char *s) {
    const char *start = s;

    /* First handle the bytes until s is aligned on a size_t boundary. */
    while ((uintptr_t)s % sizeof(size_t)) {
        if (*s == '\0')
            return s - start;
        s++;
    }

    const size_t *wptr = (const size_t *)s;

    while (1) {
        size_t word = *wptr;
        /*
         The idea of the following bit trick is that if any byte in "word" is 0,
         then subtracting ONES will cause a borrow from that byte, leaving a 1
         in the corresponding byte of (word - ONES).  Then, when we take
         ~word, those positions become 1’s if they were 0 originally.
         Finally, HIGHS contains the highest bit in every byte.
         So by checking
             (word - ONES) & ~word & HIGHS
         we can tell if any byte was zero.
        */
        if (((word - ONES) & ~word & HIGHS) != 0) {
            /* One of the bytes in this word is zero.
               Now check each byte in the word to find the exact position. */
            s = (const char *)wptr;
            for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof(size_t); i++) {
                if (s[i] == '\0')
                    return (s + i) - start;
            }
        }
        wptr++;
    }
}
---------------------------------------------------

Explanation of the code:

1. The function first advances the pointer byte‐by‐byte until it is aligned on a word boundary. This is because many platforms require aligned accesses for word‐sized loads.

2. Once aligned, it casts the pointer to a size_t pointer and reads one word at a time.

3. The bit trick:
   • Subtracting ONES from the word will “borrow” if any byte was 0.
   • Then taking ~word, a byte that was 0 becomes 0xFF.
   • ANDing with HIGHS (which has the highest bit set in each byte) will leave a nonzero result if any byte was 0.

4. When a word is found that possibly contains a zero byte, the code reverts to a simple byte‐by‐byte scan within that word to determine the exact position of the terminator.

This method is significantly faster than checking each byte one at a time on many systems, as it uses fewer memory accesses and leverages the fact that size_t is exactly the size of a word. Any correct variant implementing this idea is acceptable.

