Below is an example of how you can create a generic Result type and a helper function that wraps a given function in a try/catch block. You can also add an async version if needed.

──────────────────────────────────────────────
• Define the Result type:

──────────────────────────────────────────────
export type Result<T, E = unknown> =
  | { success: true; value: T }
  | { success: false; error: E };

──────────────────────────────────────────────
• Create a synchronous wrapper function:

──────────────────────────────────────────────
export function tryCatch<T>(fn: () => T): Result<T> {
  try {
    const value = fn();
    return { success: true, value };
  } catch (error) {
    return { success: false, error };
  }
}

──────────────────────────────────────────────
• (Optional) Create an async version if you need to handle Promise-returning functions:

──────────────────────────────────────────────
export async function tryCatchAsync<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>): Promise<Result<T>> {
  try {
    const value = await fn();
    return { success: true, value };
  } catch (error) {
    return { success: false, error };
  }
}

──────────────────────────────────────────────
• Usage examples:

──────────────────────────────────────────────
const resultSync = tryCatch(() => {
  // Some synchronous code that might throw
  return "Hello, world!";
});

if (resultSync.success) {
  console.log("Value:", resultSync.value);
} else {
  console.error("Error:", resultSync.error);
}

(async () => {
